rewriting \\(L_3\\) as \\(y = 2x+1\\) gives \\(1 = 2\\cdot2+1 =5\\), which is false.  \n  Instead we note that \\((2,1)\\) lies on the line \\(y = -\\tfrac12 x +2\\), which has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n  Replace \\(L_3\\) by \\(L_3': y = -\\tfrac12 x +2\\).  \n\nNow the three lines are  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=2x,\\qquad\nL_3':\\;y=-\\tfrac12 x+2,\n\\]\n\nall sunny and distinct, and each of the three points of \\(T_3\\) belongs to one of them.  \n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) we have a configuration with exactly \\(k=3\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Remarks on the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction used for \\(k=0\\) exploits the fact that the second coordinate of every point of \\(T_n\\) is bounded by \\(n\\).  \nFor larger values of \\(k\\) one would need to replace some of the horizontal lines by sunny lines while still covering the points that are no longer hit by the removed horizontals.  \nA natural idea is to keep \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) and to try to cover the remaining triangular set  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}\n\\]\n\nwith exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nThe difficulty lies in producing, for an arbitrary \\(k\\), a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers every point of \\(T_k'\\).  \nThe inductive attempt “add the line \\(y = x - m\\) at step \\(m\\)” fails because the new points \\((m,2),\\dots ,(2,m)\\) lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is not sunny.  \nConsequently a different pattern of slopes is required, and at present no universally valid pattern has been exhibited.\n\nTherefore the existence (or non‑existence) of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) (and for \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)) remains an open problem within this answer.  \n\n---  \n\n*The answer above establishes rigorously that \\(k=0\\) is always possible and that \\(k=n\\) is possible for the smallest non‑trivial case \\(n=3\\).  Determining the full set