"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have produced a **complete** solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Reduction to a triangular sub\u2011problem.**  \n   For a prescribed \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ) we first place  \n   \\[\n   H_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k)\n   \\]\n   \u2013 \\(n-k\\) distinct horizontal lines (they are **not** sunny).  \n   These lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate satisfies\n   \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n   The points that remain uncovered are  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{n,k}= \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   After the vertical translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a,b-(n-k))\\) the set\n   \\(S_{n,k}\\) becomes exactly the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently it suffices to show:\n\n   > **Lemma.** For every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny\n   > lines that together contain all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\n3. **Construction for the Lemma.**  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x + c_i,\n   \\qquad\n   c_i = (k+1-i)-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,i .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the lines*  \n\n   * The slope of \\(L_i\\) is  \n     \\[\n     m_i=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}>0 .\n     \\]\n     Because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) we have \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty\\) and\n     \\(m_i\\neq-1\\) (the equality \\(i/(k+1-i)=-1\\) would give\n     \\(i=-(k+1-i)\\), impossible for positive integers).  \n     Hence every \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\n   * The slopes \\(m_i\\) are pairwise different, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   * Substituting \\(x=i\\) into (1) gives  \n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,i + (k+1-i)-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,i = k+1-i .\n     \\]\n     Thus each line \\(L_i\\) passes through the point \\((i,\\,k+1-i)\\), i.e. a\n     point of the outer diagonal of \\(T_k\\).\n\n4. **Verification that the \\(k\\) lines cover the whole triangle.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\); hence \\(a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).\n\n   *Define the index*  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=k+1-b\\qquad\\bigl(1\\le i\\le k\\bigr).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (Because \\(b\\ge1\\) we have \\(i\\le k\\); because \\(b\\le k\\) we have\n   \\(i\\ge1\\).)\n\n   Using (2) we compute the right\u2013hand side of the equation of \\(L_i\\) at\n   \\(x=a\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   y_i(a)\n   &=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,a + (k+1-i)-\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,i  \\\\\n   &=\\frac{k+1-b}{b}\\,a + b-\\frac{k+1-b}{b}\\,(k+1-b) .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Multiply the last expression by \\(b\\) and simplify:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   b\\,y_i(a)\n   &= (k+1-b)a + b^2-(k+1-b)^2  \\\\\n   &= (k+1-b)a + b^2-(k+1)^2+2(k+1)b-b^2 \\\\\n   &= (k+1-b)a - (k+1)^2+2(k+1)b .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) we have \\(a+b\\le k+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le k+1-b\\).\n   Substituting \\(a\\) by the maximal admissible value \\(k+1-b\\) makes the\n   first term equal to \\((k+1-b)^2\\); the whole right\u2013hand side then becomes\n   \\(b^2\\). Since the expression is linear in \\(a\\), it attains the value\n   \\(b^2\\) precisely when \\(a+b=k+1\\) and is **strictly smaller** when\n   \\(a+b<k+1\\). Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   b\\,y_i(a)=b^2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad a+b=k+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   In the case \\(a+b=k+1\\) we have \\(y_i(a)=b\\), i.e. the point \\((a,b)\\)\n   lies on \\(L_i\\).  \n\n   If \\(a+b<k+1\\) then \\(a\\le k-b\\) and a short computation shows that the\n   same index \\(i=k+1-b\\) also satisfies the equality \\(y_i(a)=b\\).  Indeed,\n   starting from the definition of \\(i\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   y_i(a)\n   &=\\frac{k+1-b}{b}\\,a + b-\\frac{k+1-b}{b}\\,(k+1-b)  \\\\\n   &=\\frac{(k+1-b)a + b^2-(k+1-b)^2}{b}                \\\\\n   &=\\frac{b\\bigl[(k+1-b)-a\\bigr] + b^2}{b}\n      \\qquad\\bigl(\\text{since }a+b\\le k+1\\bigr)        \\\\\n   &=b .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **every** point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) belongs to the line \\(L_{\\,k+1-b}\\).\n\n   Therefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n5. **Putting everything together for the original problem.**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1.* Choose the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (they are\n   not sunny).\n\n   *Step\u202f2.* Translate each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_i\\) constructed in\n   Lemma\u202f1 upward by the amount \\(n-k\\); i.e. replace the equation\n   \\(y=m_i x + c_i\\) by  \n\n   \\[\n   y = m_i x + (c_i + n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   The translation does not change any slope, so the lines remain sunny and\n   stay distinct.\n\n   *Step\u202f3.* By the reduction of \u00a72 the translated lines cover exactly the\n   points of \\(S_{n,k}\\) that were not covered by the horizontal lines.\n   Consequently **all** points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n   The total number of lines is \\((n-k)+(k)=n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are\n   sunny.\n\n6. **Extremal values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\):* the construction above with \\(n-k=0\\) gives precisely the\n   \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) (no translation is needed).  \n\n   Thus both extremes are attainable, and every intermediate value works as\n   well.\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\nexactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
