 L:\\;y=-(n-1)x+n+1 .\n> \\]  \n> The slope \\(-\\,(n-1)\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  A direct substitution shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the horizontal lines (if its second coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\)) or on \\(L\\) (if its second coordinate equals \\(n\\)).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- **Sunny condition:** For \\(n\\ge3\\), the slope \\(-(n-1)\\le-2\\) is indeed different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and not infinite, so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n- **Coverage claim:** Points with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-1\\) are covered by the horizontal lines – correct.  \n- **Points with \\(b=n\\):** In \\(T_n\\) the only point with \\(b=n\\) is \\((1,n)\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a\\le1\\). Substituting \\((1,n)\\) into the equation of \\(L\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  y=-(n-1)\\cdot1+n+1 = -n+1+n+1 = 2,\n  \\]  \n  which equals \\(2\\), not \\(n\\) (for \\(n\\ge3\\)). Hence \\((1,n)\\notin L\\). Therefore the line \\(L\\) does **not** cover the required point, and the construction fails.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error**; the existence of a configuration with exactly one sunny line is not established.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Existence for \\(k=n\\)\n\n> “For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the sunny line  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}x+\\Bigl(n+1-i-\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}i\\Bigr).\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> … For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) put \\(i:=n+1-b\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) gives\n> \\[\n> y_i(a)=\\frac{i}{n+1-i}\\,a+\\Bigl(n+1-i-\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}i\\Bigr)\n>          =\\frac{(n+1-b)a+b^2-(n+1-b)^2}{b}=b .\n> \\]  \n> Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_{\\,n+1-b}\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- **Sunny condition:** The slope \\(\\frac{i}{n+1-i}>0\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or infinite, so each \\(L_i\\) is indeed sunny.  \n- **Algebraic verification:** Let \\(i=n+1-b\\). Then \\(n+1-i=b\\). Substituting into the expression for \\(y_i(a)\\) yields  \n  \\[\n  y_i(a)=\\frac{(n+1-b)a+b^2-(n+1-b)^2}{b}.\n  \\]  \n  For this to equal \\(b\\) we would need  \n  \\[\n  (n+1-b)a+b^2-(n+1-b)^2 = b^2,\n  \\]  \n  i.e. \\((n+1-b)a = (n+1-b)^2\\). Since \\(n+1-b\\ge1\\), this simplifies to \\(a = n+1-b\\).  \n  However, for a generic point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we only know \\(a\\le n+1-b\\); equality need not hold. Thus the derived equality holds **only** for the points on the “diagonal” \\(a=n+1-b\\).  \n\n- **Concrete counterexample:** Take \\(n=3\\). Then \\(T_3\\) contains \\((1,1)\\). Here \\(b=1\\) gives \\(i=3\\). The line \\(L_3\\) is \\(y=3x-8\\). Substituting \\((1,1)\\) gives \\(1\\neq3\\cdot1-8=-5\\). Hence \\((1,1)\\) is not on \\(L_3\\).  \n\nTherefore the family \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) does **not** cover all points of \\(T_n\\); the algebraic claim is false.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step contains a **Critical Error**; the existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is not proved.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Impossibility for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n> “Split the set \\(T_n\\) into the two colour classes  \n> \\[\n> A=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\},\\qquad\n> B=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\not\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\}.\n> \\]  \n> Every line that is **not** sunny is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); each of these three families contains only points of a single colour (all points on a horizontal line have the same parity of the second coordinate, etc.).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- **Horizontal lines:** Points on a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) have coordinates \\((a,c)\\). As \\(a\\) varies, the parity of \\(a\\) varies, while the parity of \\(c\\) is fixed. Consequently both possibilities \\(a\\equiv c\\pmod2\\) and \\(a\\not\\equiv c\\pmod2\\) occur; the line contains points from **both** \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \n- **Vertical lines:** Analogously, a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains points \\((c,b)\\) with both parities of \\(b\\).  \n- **Slope \\(-1\\) lines:** These indeed contain points of a single colour (since \\(a+b\\) is constant).  \n\nThus the claim that *each* non‑sunny line contains points of a single colour is false; it holds only for the slope \\(-1\\) family.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This statement is a **Critical Error**; the subsequent counting argument built on it is invalid.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Parity behaviour of sunny lines\n\n> “A sunny line has slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore its direction vector \\((p,q)\\) (with coprime integers \\(p,q\\)) satisfies \\(p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p\\neq -q\\).  Consequently the parity of the two coordinates changes when we move from one lattice point of the line to the next: the vector \\((p,q)\\) has one even and one odd component, so the colour of the points on a sunny line alternates.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of each colour class.”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The direction vector \\((p,q)\\) of a line with rational slope \\(p/q\\) (in lowest terms) indeed satisfies \\(p\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq0\\). However, the condition “\\(p\\neq -q\\)” only excludes slope \\(-1\\); it does **not** force one of \\(p,q\\) to be even and the other odd. For example, the line with slope \\(2/3\\) has direction vector \\((3,2)\\) (one even, one odd), but the line with slope \\(3/5\\) has direction vector \\((5,3)\\) (both odd). Both are sunny, yet the parity of the coordinates does **not** alternate: moving by \\((5,3)\\) flips both coordinates’ parity, preserving the relation \\(a\\equiv b\\pmod2\\). Consequently a sunny line can contain arbitrarily many points of the same colour class (e.g., all points of \\(A\\) on the line \\(y=2x\\)).  \n\n- The claim “a sunny line can contain at most one point of each colour class” is therefore false.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This is a **Critical Error**; the bound on the number of points of each colour that sunny lines can cover is unsupported.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Counting inequality (2)\n\n> “With \\(k\\) sunny lines we can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(A\\) and at most \\(k\\) points of \\(B\\).  The remaining points must be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, each of which can cover points only from a single colour class.  Therefore we need  \n> \\[\n> |A|-k\\le n-k\\quad\\text{and}\\quad |B|-k\\le n-k .\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \n- The premise “a sunny line can cover at most one point of each colour class” is false (see previous step). Hence the deduction that at most \\(k\\) points of each colour are covered by sunny lines is invalid.  \n\n- Moreover, the earlier claim that each non‑sunny line can cover points of only one colour class is also false (horizontal and vertical lines can cover both). Consequently the inequality (2) does not follow from the actual geometry of the problem.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This inequality is derived from false premises; the step is a **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Final inequality argument\n\n> “For \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) the left–hand sides of (2) are strictly larger than the right–hand sides (a short calculation … shows that \\(|A|-k\\ge n-k+1\\)).  Hence (2) cannot hold, contradicting the assumed existence of a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nSince inequality (2) itself is unfounded, any subsequent comparison is irrelevant. The “short calculation” does not rescue the argument because the underlying model is incorrect.  \n\n**Conclusion:** This step inherits the **Critical Error** from the previous step.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Overall conclusion\n\n> “The only values of \\(k\\) … are \\(\\{0,1,n\\}\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nBecause the existence proofs for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are invalid, and the impossibility proof for the remaining values is flawed, the conclusion is not justified.  \n\n**Conclusion:** The final answer is **unsupported**; the solution is invalid.\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains multiple **Critical Errors** that invalidate both the constructive parts (for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\)) and the impossibility argument (for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)). Consequently the claimed answer \\(\\{0,1,n\\}\\) is not established. The solution fails to meet the rigorous standards required for an IMO‑level proof."
