unny.\n\n   *\\(k=n\\).*  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}x+\\Bigl(n+1-i-\\frac{i}{\\,n+1-i\\,}i\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i}{n+1-i}\\) is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(\\infty\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(i:=n+1-b\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (1) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   y_i(a)\n   &=\\frac{n+1-b}{\\,b\\,}a+\\Bigl(b-\\frac{n+1-b}{\\,b\\,}(n+1-b)\\Bigr)  \\\\\n   &=\\frac{(n+1-b)a+b^2-(n+1-b)^2}{b}\n   =b .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{\\,n+1-b}\\); i.e. \\((a,b)\\in L_{\\,n+1-b}\\).  \n   Because every point of \\(T_n\\) determines a unique index \\(i=n+1-b\\), the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).  \n   All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, so a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n3. **Impossibility for the remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Assume that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le k\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Split \\(T_n\\) into the two colour classes  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\},\\qquad\n   B=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\not\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines.*  \n   A non‑sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  \n   In each of these three families the parity of the two coordinates is **constant** along the line:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{family} & a\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\\\ \\hline\n   y=c & a\\equiv c\\pmod 2\\\\\n   x=c & b\\equiv c\\pmod 2\\\\\n   x+y=c & a+b\\equiv c\\pmod 2 .\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n   Consequently a non‑sunny line can contain points **only from one of the tw