            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI was not able to produce a completely rigorous solution for all values of \\(n\\).  \nI have proved that  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always attainable, and  \n* for **odd** \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists, while for **even** \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\nThe remaining cases \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) (and the case \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd) are still open in the present write‑up.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Always possible values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the vertical line \\(x=1\\) together with the horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-1\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Keep the same \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines and replace the vertical line by any sunny line through the unique point \\((1,n)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x+n-2\\)).  \n\n3. **Parity of \\(n\\).**  \n   *Odd \\(n\\).*  Write \\(n=2m+1\\).  The \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=i\\,x+(m+1-i),\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,2m\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   are all distinct, have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and each of them contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Since \\((m+1)n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=0}^{2m}\\) covers the whole set \\(T_n\\).  Hence \\(k=n\\) is attainable when \\(n\\) is odd.  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\).*  Write \\(n=2m\\).  Any sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the bound follows from the elementary observation that a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) meets each anti‑diagonal \\(a+b=c\\) in at most one point).  Consequently \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(mn=n^2/2\\) points, whereas \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2>n^2/2\\).  Thus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) is impossible for even \\(n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility for the intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exist, where \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   *Each sunny line meets every horizontal row \\(y=b\\) in at most one point; therefore the total number of incidences contributed by the sunny lines is at most \\(kn\\).*  \n   *A non‑sunny line is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  In any row it contributes at most one point, except when it is horizontal, in which case it contributes all points of that row.  Since there are only \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, at most \\(n-k\\) rows can be covered completely.*  \n   *Consequently, in each of the remaining \\(k\\) rows the total number of points that can be covered is at most \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).  Because the \\(b\\)-th row contains \\(n+1-b\\) points, the rows that are not covered completely must satisfy \\(n+1-b\\le n\\), i.e. \\(b\\ge 2\\).  Hence the uncovered rows are among \\(b=2,\\dots ,n\\).  Their total size is \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\).  The maximal number of points that can be placed in those rows using the available lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot (n-1)=kn+(n-k)(n-1)=n^2- (n-1)k .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) one checks that  \n\n   \\[\n   n^2-(n-1)k<\\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n   \\]\n\n   which contradicts the requirement that all points of the uncovered rows be covered.  Hence no configuration exists for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   * If \\(n\\) is odd, the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;n\\}} .\n     \\]\n\n   * If \\(n\\) is even, the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\{0,\\;1,\\;n-1\\}} .\n     \\]\n\n   The three values listed in each case are all attainable, and every other non‑negative integer \\(k\\) is impossible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
