h \\(b\\le n-1\\) are covered by the horizontal line \\(y=b\\). Hence the covering condition holds. The sunny line is not parallel to the axes nor to \\(x+y=0\\), so exactly one line is sunny.  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Parity of \\(n\\)\n\n#### (a) Odd \\(n\\)  \n\n> “Write \\(n=2m+1\\). The \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> L_i:\\;y=i\\,x+(m+1-i),\\qquad i=0,1,\\dots ,2m\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]\n> are all distinct, have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and each of them contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n*Verification of distinctness and point count:*  \n- For each integer \\(i\\) the line is well‑defined and distinct.  \n- Substituting \\(i=0\\) gives \\(y=m+1\\), a horizontal line. Its slope is \\(0\\), which **is** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis. Hence \\(L_0\\) is **not** sunny.  \n\nBecause the definition of a sunny line explicitly excludes horizontal lines, the claim “the \\(n\\) sunny lines … are all distinct, have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)” is false. Consequently the construction does **not** provide \\(n\\) sunny lines; it provides only \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (all except \\(L_0\\)).  \n\n*Impact on the conclusion:* The statement “\\(k=n\\) is attainable when \\(n\\) is odd” relies on the erroneous claim that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Since the construction fails, the existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\) for odd \\(n\\) is not established.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the construction includes a non‑sunny line, invalidating the claim that \\(k=n\\) is attainable for odd \\(n\\).\n\n#### (b) Even \\(n\\)  \n\n> “Write \\(n=2m\\). Any sunny line contains at most \\(m\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the bound follows from the elementary observation that a line of slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) meets each anti‑diagonal \\(a+b=c\\) in at most one point). Consequently \\(n\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(mn=n^{2}/2\\) points, whereas \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2>n^{2}/2\\).”\n\n*Verification of the