)k .\n> \\]  \n> For \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) one checks that  \n> \\[\n> n^{2}-(n-1)k<\\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n> \\]  \n> which contradicts the requirement that all points of the uncovered rows be covered. Hence no configuration exists for any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).”\n\nWe examine each claim.\n\n#### 4.1 Sunny‑line incidence bound  \n\nThe statement “each sunny line meets every horizontal row in at most one point” is correct (a non‑horizontal line can intersect a given horizontal line at most once). Hence the total number of incidences contributed by the \\(k\\) sunny lines is indeed at most \\(kn\\).  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n#### 4.2 Non‑sunny lines covering whole rows  \n\nThe description of non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)) is accurate. A horizontal line does cover an entire row; a vertical or slope \\(-1\\) line can contribute at most one point per row. Since there are \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, at most \\(n-k\\) rows can be fully covered.  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n#### 4.3 “Rows that are not covered completely must satisfy \\(b\\ge2\\)”  \n\nThe argument: “Because the \\(b\\)-th row contains \\(n+1-b\\) points, the rows that are not covered completely must satisfy \\(n+1-b\\le n\\), i.e. \\(b\\ge2\\).”  \n\n- For **any** row, \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) holds (equality for \\(b=1\\)). Thus the inequality does **not** exclude row \\(1\\).  \n- Consequently the deduction that uncovered rows lie only among \\(b=2,\\dots ,n\\) is **invalid**. Row \\(1\\) could also be among the uncovered rows.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the exclusion of row \\(1\\) is unjustified.\n\n#### 4.4 Upper bound on points in uncovered rows  \n\nThe solution claims the maximal number of points that can be placed in the uncovered rows is  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot (n-1)=n^{2}-(n-1)k .\n\\]\n\nThis bound is derived under the (incorrect) assumption that each fully covered row contributes at most \\(n-1\\) points. In reality, a fully covered row may be