roved.  \n- The impossibility of all intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) (or \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) for even \\(n\\)) is based on the erroneous counting argument of Step 4, so the claim of completeness is unfounded.\n\n**Issues:**  \n- **Critical Error** for the odd‑\\(n\\) case (unsupported existence of \\(k=n\\)).  \n- **Justification Gap** for the even‑\\(n\\) case (no construction for \\(k=n-1\\)).  \n- **Justification Gap** regarding the completeness of the answer (the impossibility proof is invalid).\n\n---\n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution contains multiple critical logical and factual mistakes (incorrect construction for odd \\(n\\), false counting arguments, and an outright false inequality) and leaves several existence claims without proof. Consequently the answer cannot be accepted as correct."
