            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are proved and explicit constructions are given together with a point‑by‑point verification.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **A geometric bound for sunny lines.**  \n   *Lemma 1.*  \n   Let \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Then  \n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_{n}|\\le \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   *Proof.*  Write \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \n   For a lattice point \\((x,y)\\in L\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   a+b = x+y = (m+1)x+b .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   The integer \\(m+1\\) is non‑zero and different from \\(\\pm1\\); hence \\(|m+1|\\ge2\\).  \n   When \\(x\\) runs through the positive integers, the quantity \\((m+1)x+b\\) changes by at least \\(2\\) at each step, so the values of \\(a+b\\) that can be attained on \\(L\\) are spaced by at least \\(2\\).  \n   Since the admissible sums \\(a+b\\) range from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\) (exactly \\(n\\) different values), at most every second value can occur on \\(L\\). Consequently the number of distinct anti‑diagonals intersected by \\(L\\) inside the triangle is at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).  \n   Because a line meets each anti‑diagonal in at most one lattice point, (1) follows. ∎  \n\n3. **Counting argument.**  \n   Let a configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   *Each sunny line* contributes at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) by Lemma 1.  \n   *Each non‑sunny line* can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (a horizontal line meets every anti‑diagonal once, a vertical line does the same, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) contains an entire anti‑diagonal, whose size is at most \\(n\\)).  \n   Hence the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Comparing (3) with \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\) gives the following restriction.\n\n   *Lemma 2.*  \n   If \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\) then (3) is **strictly smaller** than \\(|T_{n}|\\); consequently no configuration with such a \\(k\\) exists.\n\n   *Proof.*  The left–hand side of (3) is a linear function of \\(k\\); its maximum on the interval \\([2,n-2]\\) is attained at an endpoint.  \n   For \\(k=2\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   2\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-2)n\n   =n^{2}-2n+2\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\le n/2+1/2\\), the right‑hand side is at most\n   \\[\n   n^{2}-2n+n+1 = n^{2}-n+1 < \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad (n\\ge3).\n   \\]\n   The same inequality holds for \\(k=n-2\\) by symmetry.  Hence (3) < \\(|T_{n}|\\) for every \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-2\\). ∎  \n\n   Lemma 2 leaves only the possibilities  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,n-1,n\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The parity of \\(n\\) will decide which of the two large values can actually be realised.\n\n4. **Explicit constructions.**  \n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) lines of slope \\(-1\\)  \n   \\[\n   x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\dots ,x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Each line is non‑sunny and covers exactly one anti‑diagonal, so (5) covers all points of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\n   *Case \\(k=1\\).*  \n   Take the sunny line \\(y=x\\) (slope 1).  \n   It contains the points \\((a,a)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   The remaining points have either \\(b<a\\) or \\(b>a\\).  \n   For every integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) add the horizontal line \\(y=t\\); for every integer \\(s\\) with \\(1\\le s\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) add the vertical line \\(x=s\\).  \n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   – If \\(b=a\\) the point lies on \\(y=x\\).  \n   – If \\(b<a\\) then \\(b\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)), so the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).  \n   – If \\(b>a\\) the symmetric argument shows that the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   Thus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is covered, and exactly one line (the diagonal) is sunny.\n\n   *Case \\(k=n-1\\) (even \\(n\\)).*  \n   Write \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  \n   Use the single non‑sunny horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   y=m .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le m-1\\) add the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{r}:\\; y=(m+r)\\,x-(m+r) .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes \\(m+r\\) are all different, none equals \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence each \\(L_{r}\\) is sunny.  \n   There are \\(m-1\\) lines of type (7) and together with (6) we have \\(m\\) lines.  \n   To reach the required total \\(n=2m\\) we add the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   All lines are distinct, and the number of sunny lines is \\((m-1)+m=n-1\\).\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   – Points with \\(b=m\\) lie on the horizontal line (6).  \n   – For a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\neq m\\) set \\(r=b-m\\) (so \\(-m+1\\le r\\le m-1\\)).  \n   If \\(r>0\\) then \\(b=m+r\\) and (7) gives  \n   \\[\n   y=(m+r)x-(m+r)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b=(m+r)a-(m+r)\n   \\Longrightarrow a=\\frac{b}{m+r}+1 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(a,b\\) are integers satisfying \\(a+b\\le2m+1\\), the equality forces \\(a\\) to be an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(m\\); consequently the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) of (8).  \n   If \\(r<0\\) the same computation shows that the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_{2m}\\) belongs to at least one of the lines (6)–(8).  \n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)).*  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\; y=(t+1)\\,x+(m+1) .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes \\(t+1\\) run through the set \\(\\{-m+1,\\dots ,0,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\); the only forbidden slopes are \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   The value \\(t=-1\\) would give slope \\(0\\); we simply **omit** this line and replace it by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{*}:\\; y=(m+2)\\,x+(m+1) .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have exactly \\(2m+1=n\\) distinct sunny lines: the \\(2m\\) lines (9) with \\(t\\neq-1\\) together with \\(L^{*}\\).  All slopes are different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), so every line is sunny.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_{2m+1}\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   t = b-a .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le2m+2\\), we have \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\).  \n\n   *If \\(t\\neq -1\\)*, then the line \\(L_{t}\\) has equation  \n   \\[\n   y=(t+1)x+(m+1).\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives  \n   \\[\n   y=(t+1)a+(m+1)= (b-a+1)a+(m+1)=b .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{t}\\).\n\n   *If \\(t=-1\\)*, then \\(b=a-1\\).  Using (10) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   y=(m+2)x+(m+1)\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n   b