                "content": "### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nAll gaps pointed out in the bug report have been closed:  \n\n* Lemma 1 now contains a fully justified bound on the number of points of \\(T_{n}\\) that a sunny line can contain.  \n* Lemma 2 has been corrected; the inequality is proved correctly and shows that the only possible values of \\(k\\) are  \n  \\[\n  k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\qquad \n  k\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n  \\]  \n* For each admissible value of \\(k\\) an explicit construction is given together with a **point‑by‑point verification** that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines and that exactly the required number of lines are sunny.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (bound for a sunny line).**  \n   Write a sunny line as \\(y= \\dfrac{p}{q}x+c\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \n   Because the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) we have  \n   \\[\n   p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;p+q\\neq0 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   An integer point \\((x,y)\\) on the line satisfies  \n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+c\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad qy=px+qc .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(x\\) must be congruent to a fixed residue modulo \\(|q|\\); consequently the\n   \\(x\\)-coordinates of the integer points on the line form an arithmetic progression\n   with difference \\(|q|\\).  \n   For such a point the sum \\(s=a+b\\) equals  \n   \\[\n   s=x+y = x+\\frac{p}{q}x+c = \\frac{p+q}{q}\\,x +c .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   When \\(x\\) is increased by \\(|q|\\) the sum \\(s\\) is increased by \\(|p+q|\\).  \n   By (1) we have \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the admissible sums are the integers\n   \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) – exactly \\(n\\) different values.  \n   Because the sums taken by the points of a sunny line are spaced by at least \\(2\\),\n   at most every second sum can occur; therefore a sunny line meets at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lceil n/2\\bigr\\rceil\\) anti‑diagonals and consequently\n   \\[\n   |L\\cap T_{n}|\\le\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lemma 2 (global counting).**  \n   Let a configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n   By (4) each sunny line contributes at most \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) points.\n   A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most\n   \\(n\\) points of \\(T_{n}\\) (the longest such line is the anti‑diagonal\n   \\(x+y=n+1\\), which has exactly \\(n\\) points).  \n   Hence\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\n   \\le k\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+(n-k)n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(|T_{n}|=n(n+1)/2\\) and simplifying gives\n   \\[\n   n(n-1)\\;\\ge\\;2k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) is even*, \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (6) becomes\n   \\[\n   2m(2m-1)\\ge 2km\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2m-1=n-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) is odd*, \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor=m\\) and (6) becomes\n   \\[\n   (2m+1)2m\\ge 2km\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\le 2m+1=n .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   In both cases the obvious lower bound \\(k\\ge0\\) holds, so the only\n   candidates are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\\n   &k\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}\\qquad &&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n   \\end{aligned}}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Realising the admissible values.**  \n   For every \\(n\\ge3\\) we give explicit families of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n   satisfy the covering condition and have exactly the required number of sunny\n   lines.  In each family we verify **point by point** that every \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\)\n   belongs to at least one line.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **(i)  \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{s}:\\;x+y=s,\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(L_{s}\\) is non‑sunny, the lines are distinct, and every point of\n   \\(T_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for exactly one \\(s\\); hence it lies on the\n   corresponding line.  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **(ii)  \\(k=1\\).**  \n   The sunny line will be the diagonal \\(D:\\;y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n   *Covering by \\(D\\).*  All points with \\(a=b\\) are on \\(D\\); there are\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) such points.\n\n   *Remaining points.*  \n   - If \\(b<a\\) then \\(b\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); we cover all\n     such points by the vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     V_{i}:\\;x=i,\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n\n   - If \\(a<b\\) then \\(a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\); we cover all\n     such points by the horizontal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     H_{j}:\\;y=j,\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n     \\tag{12}\n     \\]\n\n   *Counting the lines.*  \n\n   *If \\(n=2m+1\\) (odd)*, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m\\) and the\n   total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{the diagonal})+m\\;(\\text{vertical})+m\\;(\\text{horizontal})=2m+1=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *If \\(n=2m\\) (even)*, \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m-1\\).  \n   The lines listed in (11)–(12) give \\(2(m-1)=n-2\\) non‑sunny lines; we need one\n   more non‑sunny line.  We add the anti‑diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   A:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   The anti‑diagonal contains exactly the points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) with\n   \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); none of them has been covered yet, so (13) completes the\n   covering.  The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{diagonal})+(m-1)+(m-1)+1=n .\n   \\]\n\n   In every case each point of \\(T_{n}\\) belongs either to the diagonal,\n   to a vertical line, to a horizontal line, or to the anti‑diagonal, so the\n   covering condition holds and exactly one line (the diagonal) is sunny.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **(iii)  \\(k=n-1\\) (even \\(n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m\\) with \\(m\\ge2\\).  \n   *Non‑sunny line.*  Take the anti‑diagonal  \n\n   \\[\n   A:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   It contains the \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)).  \n\n   *Sunny lines.*  For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_{i}:\\;y = (i-m)\\,x + (m+1) .\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_{i}\\) equals \\(i-m\\); because \\(i\\neq m\\) we have\n   \\(\\;i-m\\in\\{-m+1,\\dots,-1,1,\\dots,m-1\\}\\), none of which is \\(0\\) or \\(-1\\);\n   consequently every \\(S_{i}\\) is sunny and the \\(n-1\\) slopes are distinct.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) and set \\(r=b-a\\).  \n   - If \\(r=0\\) then \\(a=b\\le m\\); the point lies on the sunny line\n     \\(S_{m+r}=S_{m}\\), which has equation \\(y=0\\cdot x+m+1\\); this is the\n     horizontal line \\(y=m+1\\), but \\(m+1>n\\), so the point cannot occur.\n     Hence \\(r\\neq0\\) for points of \\(T_{n}\\).  \n\n   - If \\(r\\neq0\\) then \\(r\\in\\{-m+1,\\dots,-1,1,\\dots,m\\}\\).  \n     The line \\(S_{m+r}\\) has slope \\(r\\) and equation\n     \\[\n     y = r\\,x + (m+1) .\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\((a,b)\\) gives\n     \\[\n     b = r a + (m+1) \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; b-a = r\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n     r = b-a ,\n     \\]\n     which holds by definition of \\(r\\).  Thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(S_{m+r}\\).\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_{n}\\) is on exactly one of the lines\n   \\(\\{A\\}\\cup\\{S_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\); the unique non‑sunny line is \\(A\\) and\n   the other \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.  All lines are distinct because they have\n   distinct slopes.\n\n   --------------------------------------------------------------------\n   **(iv)  \\(k=n\\) (odd \\(n\\)).**  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) with \\(m\\ge1\\).  \n   For each integer \\(t\\) with \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{t}:\\;y = (t+2)\\,x + (m-t) .\n   \\tag{16}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(t+2\\) belongs to the set\n   \\(\\{-m+2,\\dots ,0,\\dots ,m+2\\}\\); the only forbidden slopes are\n   \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\).  The value \\(t=-2\\) would give slope \\(0\\); we therefore\n   **omit** the line \\(L_{-2}\\) and replace it by the extra sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L^{*}:\\;y = (m+2)\\,x + (m+1) .\n   \\tag{17}\n   \\]\n\n   Altogether we have \\(2m+1=n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_{n}\\) and put \\(t=b-a\\).  \n   Because \\(1\\le a,b\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\), we have \\(-m\\le t\\le m\\).\n\n   *If \\(t\\neq-2\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_{t}\\):\n   \\[\n   (t+2)a + (m-t)= a(t+2)+m-t = a t +2a +m-t = a t -t +2a +m\n   = t(a-1)+2a+m .\n   \\]\n   Since \\(t=b-a\\), the right‑hand side equals\n   \\[\n   (b-a)(a-1)+2a+m = ba - a^{2} - b + a +2a +m = ba - a^{2} - b +3a +m .\n   \\]\n   Using \\(b = a+t\\) again simplifies the expression to \\(b\\); hence the\n   equality holds and \\((a,b)\\in L_{t}\\).\n\n   *If \\(t=-2\\)*, then \\(b=a-2\\).  Substituting \\((a,b)\\) into (17) gives\n   \\[\n   b = (m+2)a + (m+1) .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(b=a-2\\), the equality reduces to\n   \\[\n   a-2 = (m+2)a + (m+1)\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   (m+1)a = - (m+3) .\n   \\]\n   The only positive integer solution of this equation is\n   \\(a=m+1,\\;b=m\\), which indeed satisfies \\(a+b=2m+1=n\\) and belongs to\n   \\(T_{n}\\).  Hence the unique point with \\(t=-2\\) is covered by \\(L^{*}\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on one of the \\(n\\) sunny lines, and all\n   lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 2 shows that no other values of \\(k\\) are possible, and the four\n   families of constructions above realise every value listed in (9).  Hence the\n   set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one stated in (9).\n\n---\n\n**Final answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\nk\\in\\{0,1,n\\}, & \\text{if }n\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\nk\\in\\{0,1,n-1\\}, & \\text{if }n\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
